Why: A function whose flow runs past the last mapped byte fails whole, with
"Bytes at ram:0x... are not mapped": FlowInfo::processInstruction stops the
flow at an undecodable instruction (BadDataError) with halt_baddata(), but a
DataUnavailError from the load image propagates out of the decompilation. Any
program ending with Linux's exit system call does this, since the decompiler
cannot know that syscall does not return: flow continues after it, past the
end of its segment, as in the smallest x86-64 programs written by hand. Ghidra
behaves the same when its own load image has no bytes there ("GHIDRA has no
data in the loadimage"). This stops the flow at the first unmapped byte as at
bad instruction data, with warnings of its own; a function whose entry is
unmapped still fails, having nothing to decompile. It cannot be done in host/:
the load image cannot tell an instruction's bytes from data the analysis reads
(jump tables, constant pointers), whose callers catch DataUnavailError
themselves.
Upstream: not reported; 12.1.4's FlowInfo::processInstruction catches only
UnimplError and BadDataError.

--- a/cpp/flow.cc
+++ b/cpp/flow.cc
@@ -455,6 +455,20 @@
       }
     }
   }
+  catch(DataUnavailError &err) {	// Flow ran into bytes nothing maps (decompiler-js patch)
+    if ((flags & error_unimplemented)!=0 || curaddr == data.getAddress())
+      throw err;		// rethrow: a function whose entry is unmapped has nothing to decompile
+    else {
+      // Stop the flow there, as at bad instruction data
+      step = 1;			// Pretend size 1
+      artificialHalt(curaddr,PcodeOp::badinstruction);
+      data.warning("Unmapped bytes - Truncating control flow here",curaddr);
+      if (!hasBadData()) {
+	flags |= baddata_present;
+	data.warningHeader("Control flow encountered unmapped bytes");
+      }
+    }
+  }
   VisitStat &stat(visited[curaddr]); // Mark that we visited this instruction
   stat.size = step;		// Record size of instruction
 
